\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Emotion recognition through EEG (Electroencephalography) is an emerging
 field that combines neuroscience, psychology, and artificial intelligence,
 with significant potential applications in healthcare, entertainment, and
 human-computer interaction.
 EEG measures electrical activity in the brain through non-invasive scalp
 electrodes, capturing data that reflects cognitive and emotional states.
 This method provides valuable insights into brain function, making it an
 important tool for understanding human emotions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The growing interest in EEG-based emotion recognition is driven by its potential
 to enhance our interaction with technology.
 In healthcare, it can result in advanced diagnostic tools for mental health
 conditions like depression and anxiety, allowing for more personalized
 treatment plans.
 In human-computer interaction, it promises to revolutionize how we interact
 with devices by creating systems that adapt to our emotional states in
 real-time.
 For instance, adaptive learning platforms can tailor educational content
 based on a learner's emotional engagement, while gaming systems can adjust
 difficulty levels to maintain player engagement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent advancements in machine learning and deep learning have significantly
 improved the accuracy and robustness of emotion recognition systems.
 Techniques such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural
 networks (RNNs), including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, have
 shown promise in effectively classifying emotional states from EEG signals.
 Additionally, the integration of multimodal data, such as combining EEG
 with other physiological signals, has been explored to enhance the reliability
 of emotion recognition models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, several challenges persist in this domain.
 The variability in EEG signals across different individuals and sessions
 poses a significant hurdle, often necessitating advanced preprocessing
 and feature extraction techniques.
 Moreover, the development of algorithms that generalize well across diverse
 datasets remains an ongoing research focus.
 Addressing these challenges is crucial for the deployment of practical
 and scalable EEG-based emotion recognition systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary, EEG-based emotion recognition is a promising area of research
 with the potential to innovate technology and healthcare.
 This thesis aims to explore advanced methods to improve the accuracy and
 applicability of these systems.
 Through rigorous experimentation and analysis, this work seeks to address
 existing challenges and contribute to the ongoing development of EEG technology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Motivation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Motivation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The motivation for this research stems from the need to enhance the accuracy
 and applicability of EEG-based emotion recognition systems.
 Traditional datasets and models often face limitations due to the inherent
 variability and noise in EEG data, which can significantly impact the performan
ce of emotion recognition systems.
 By creating a custom dataset, EmotionWave, and employing advanced neural
 network architectures, this thesis aims to address these challenges.
 The use of GANs for data augmentation aims to increase the dataset size
 and diversity, potentially improving model robustness and generalization.
 The ultimate goal is to develop an emotion recognition system that can
 be reliably used in real-world applications, from healthcare to human-computer
 interaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Objective
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Objective"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary objective of this thesis is to develop a robust and accurate
 EEG-based emotion recognition system.
 To achieve this, the following specific objectives are set:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Create a custom EEG dataset, EmotionWave, using the EMOTIV EPOC X headset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Augment the dataset using Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) to increase
 its size and diversity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Adapt and optimize the Joint Contrastive Learning framework with Feature
 Alignment (JCFA) for the 14-channel configuration of the EPOC X headset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conduct extensive experimentation to fine-tune the model, ensuring it effectivel
y captures the emotional states from the EEG signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Evaluate the model's performance using both the custom dataset and a baseline
 dataset (SEED) to compare and analyze results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Proposed Solution
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Proposed-Solution"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This work proposes a novel approach to EEG-based emotion recognition by
 adapting a model inspired by the Joint Contrastive Learning framework with
 Feature Alignment (JCFA) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "JCFA"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to the EMOTIV EPOC X headset.
 The primary goal is to optimize this model for the EPOC X's hardware constraint
s and fine-tune it to effectively use the 14 EEG channels provided by the
 headset.
 Additionally, a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) was employed to augment
 the dataset created using the EPOC X, increasing its size and diversity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model is designed to extract robust representations from EEG signals
 by jointly learning from the time and frequency domains through self-supervised
 learning.
 This approach employs contrastive learning to align embeddings from these
 domains into a shared latent space, improving emotion recognition accuracy.
 This research builds upon this architecture, applying it to work with the
 EPOC X's 14-channel configuration.
 Additionally, hyper-parameter tuning experiments, detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Hyper-parameter Tuning Experiments"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, were important in fine-tuning the training process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dataset Creation and Augmentation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dataset creation process, detailed in Section 
