, involved recording EEG data using the EMOTIV EPOC X headset, which provides
 14 EEG channels (AF3, F7, F3, FC5, T7, P7, O1, O2, P8, T8, FC6, F4, F8,
 AF4) at a sampling rate of 200 SPS (Samples Per Second).
 Four separate experiments were conducted, each lasting approximately 55
 minutes.
 The experimental protocol included:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Instructions for the participant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Headset calibration
\series default
 (15s with eyes open looking at one spot, 15s with eyes closed)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Each trial consisting of:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
5s hint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
2-4 minute video clip intended to elicit a specific emotion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Self-assessment by the participant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
15s rest period
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the EmotivPRO Builder, the EEG data were automatically marked with
 events such as the start and stop of each video.
 The order of the emotions presented was positive, negative, neutral, and
 then repeating in this sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To enhance the dataset, a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) was employed
 to augment the recorded data, increasing the dataset size and diversity.
 This augmented dataset was used alongside the original recordings to train
 the model.
 For comparison, the model was also trained on the SEED dataset, which includes
 a different set of EEG recordings and labeling conventions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pre-training
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pre-training phase, detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:ModelArchitecture - PreTraining"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, focused on learning generalizable representations from the EEG data without
 using labeled information.
 This phase aimed to initialize the model with features that effectively
 capture the underlying patterns in EEG signals.
 The key steps in the pre-training phase included:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Data Augmentation:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Time Domain:
\series default
 Gaussian noise added to the original time-based signals to generate weakly
 augmented samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Frequency Domain:
\series default
 Original time-based signals transformed into the frequency spectrum using
 Fourier Transformation, with perturbations introduced by adding or removing
 frequency components.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Contrastive Encoders:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Time-based Encoder (ET):
\series default
 A 2-layer transformer processing time-domain signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Frequency-based Encoder (EF):
\series default
 A 2-layer transformer processing frequency-domain signals.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Cross-Space Projectors:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Time-based Projector (PT):
\series default
 Maps time-based embeddings to a shared latent space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Frequency-based Projector (PF):
\series default
 Maps frequency-based embeddings to the same space, ensuring the features
 extracted from both domains are comparable.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Loss Functions:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Time Domain Loss (LT):
\series default
 NT-Xent loss measuring the similarity between augmented and original time-based
 signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Frequency Domain Loss (LF):
\series default
 NT-Xent loss measuring the similarity between augmented and original frequency-
based signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Alignment Loss (LA):
\series default
 Ensures coherence between time and frequency embeddings within the shared
 latent space.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Fine-Tuning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the fine-tuning phase, detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:ModelArchitecture - FineTuning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the model was trained with labeled EEG data to optimize its performance
 for emotion recognition.
 This phase incorporated the spatial relationships between EEG channels,
 leveraging a Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) to enhance spatial feature
 extraction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
GCN Construction:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Adjacency Matrix:
\series default
 Created an adjacency matrix using cosine similarity between EEG channels
 to capture spatial features by representing the relationships between different
 EEG channels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Chebyshev Graph Convolution:
\series default
 Employed K-order Chebyshev graph convolution to extract these spatial features
 more effectively.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Emotion Classifier: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A 3-layer fully connected network with hidden layers of dimensions 1024
 and 128, followed by an output layer corresponding to the number of emotion
 classes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Loss Functions:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Cross-Space Losses (LT, LF, LA):
\series default
 NT-Xent losses applied similarly as in the pre-training phase.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Classification Loss:
\series default
 Cross-entropy loss for training the emotion classifier to minimize the
 error in predicting the correct emotion classes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
