\begin_layout Subsection
Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In recent years, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "GAN"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 have gained significant traction in deep learning.
 A GAN consists of two main components: the generator 
\begin_inset Formula $(G)$
\end_inset

 and the discriminator 
\begin_inset Formula $(D)$
\end_inset

, which are trained in opposition to each other.
 The discriminator's role is to differentiate between real and generated
 (fake) data, while the generator aims to produce fake data that is indistinguis
hable from real data to the discriminator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discriminator (
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

) is trained to accurately classify input data as real or generated.
 Conversely, the generator 
\begin_inset Formula $(G)$
\end_inset

 receives a latent noise variable 
\begin_inset Formula $(z)$
\end_inset

 as input and strives to create data that the discriminator cannot easily
 classify as fake.
 The training objective involves minimizing the following function for the
 generator and maximizing it for the discriminator:
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
[ 
\backslash
min_G 
\backslash
max_D V(G, D) = 
\backslash
mathbb{E}_{x 
\backslash
sim P_{
\backslash
text{data}}}[
\backslash
log D(x)] + 
\backslash
mathbb{E}_{z 
\backslash
sim P_z(z)}[
\backslash
log (1 - D(G(z)))].
 
\backslash
]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This objective function ensures that the discriminator becomes increasingly
 proficient at distinguishing real data from fake data, while the generator
 continuously enhances its ability to produce realistic data.
\end_layout

