\begin_layout Subsection
K-order Chebyshev Graph Convolution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The K-order Chebyshev graph convolution is a technique used in graph signal
 processing to extend convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to operate on
 graph-structured data.
 This method uses Chebyshev polynomials of the graph Laplacian to design
 filters that are localized and computationally efficient 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Chebyshev"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The graph Laplacian, denoted as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
(L
\backslash
)
\end_layout

\end_inset

, is an operator in spectral graph theory characterized by its eigenvalues
 and eigenvectors, which form the graph Fourier basis.
 Convolution on a graph is defined in the spectral domain using the graph
 Fourier transform.
 However, directly applying spectral methods can be computationally expensive
 due to eigenvalue decomposition and matrix multiplications, which scale
 quadratically with the number of graph nodes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Chebyshev"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To overcome these challenges, the K-order Chebyshev polynomial approximation
 provides a more scalable solution.
 The convolutional filter is parameterized as a polynomial of the Laplacian:
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
[ g_
\backslash
theta(
\backslash
