\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Invasive Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Intracortical Microelectrodes (IM) or Intracortical Neuron Recording (INR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intracortical microelectrodes (IM), also known as intracortical neuron recording
 (INR), have been a significant focus in neuroscience and clinical research.
 These electrodes are capable of recording rapidly changing action potentials
 (spikes) from neurons, which are the fundamental units of neural electrical
 activity.
 The high-quality recording of these signals is critical for accurate neuronal
 activity evaluation, with signal quality often assessed through metrics
 such as the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), long-term recording stability,
 and single-unit recording capability 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Intracortical_Micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the key challenges in the development and application of IMs is achieving
 long-term stability and biocompatibility.
 Long-term stability is essential for chronic implantation, where the electrode
 needs to remain stable in the extracellular fluid environment and resist
 degradation factors like cracking, delamination, peeling, and corrosion.
 The choice of materials and processing methods play a crucial role in maintaini
ng this stability.
 Biocompatibility is another essential requirement, as the electrode materials
 must not elicit a significant immune response from the surrounding tissue
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Intracortical_Micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tissue response to implanted electrodes can severely affect signal recording
 quality.
 This response is categorized into acute and chronic immune responses, primarily
 driven by mechanical damage from electrode insertion and prolonged exposure.
 Innovative solutions, such as increasing electrode compliance, soft tethering,
 and reducing the electrode's physical footprint, have been proposed to
 mitigate these effects.
 Additionally, enhancing electrode softness through biocompatible coatings,
 incorporating anti-inflammatory drugs, bioactive molecules, and certain
 cells can alleviate inflammatory responses and promote neuronal growth
 towards the electrodes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Intracortical_Micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary, intracortical microelectrodes are pivotal in recording neuronal
 activity with high fidelity, offering potential in various neuroscientific
 and clinical applications.
 However, challenges in long-term stability, biocompatibility, and tissue
 response management must be addressed to optimize their functionality and
 efficacy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Semi-Invasive Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Electrocorticography (ECoG)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Electrocorticography (ECoG) is an advanced brain imaging technique employed
 in brain-computer interface (BCI) research.
 Unlike non-invasive methods, ECoG involves surgically implanted electrodes
 to capture local synaptic field potentials of neuronal populations, making
 it an invasive procedure.
 This technique offers high spatial and temporal resolution of approximately
 milliseconds, and excellent signal quality with minimal noise 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG,SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
ECoG's proficiency is not confined to speech BCI but extends to a broader
 spectrum of applications, including the decoding of complex mental states
 such as intention decoding from motor areas and speech production 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Its capabilities in neural speech decoding include encompassing semantic,
 auditory, and articulatory representations.
 Semantic decoding interprets concepts or words in the early stages of speech
 production across various brain regions.
 Auditory decoding focuses on acoustic waveforms and spectrotemporal features,
 requiring recordings from auditory association cortices.
 Articulatory decoding captures phonation and gestures from the vocal tract
 and mouth muscles, predominantly in later speech production stages in areas
 like Broca's area and the ventral sensorimotor cortex (vSMC) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Significantly, ECoG employs somatotopic mapping in the motor cortex, extending
 its utility to speech articulators.
 This is exemplified in the vSMC, where ECoG can distinguish phonetic speech
 segments based on articulatory features, underlining its potential for
 real-time neural speech decoding.
 The vSMC's role in speech perception suggests its involvement in envisioning
 speech intention 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Comparative analysis reveals that ECoG offers several advantages over Electroenc
ephalography (EEG).
 EEG is known for its excellent temporal resolution and ability to cover
 most of the brain surface.
 However, it suffers from poor spatial resolution and signal quality, particular
ly for high-frequency activity, due to the distance of electrodes from the
 cortex 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In contrast, ECoG stands out with its unrivaled spatial and temporal resolution
, achieved through small, closely spaced electrodes, enabling high-precision
 measurement of neural-population activity.
 ECoG's superior decoding capabilities are crucial for complex tasks like
 speech waveform reconstruction, as it can record activity from various
 brain areas simultaneously and with high quality 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This is especially relevant in scenarios such as multi-task classifications
 involving complex patterns like visual stimuli recognition, where ECoG
 has been observed to outperform EEG 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The efficiency of ECoG is also influenced by electrode deployment.
 The overall accuracy of ECoG systems improves with an increased number
 of electrodes, yet this improvement plateaus beyond eight regularly distributed
 electrodes.
 This highlights a balance in electrode distribution efficiency essential
 for optimizing ECoG's performance in various tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chronic ECoG safety and efficacy studies in animals demonstrate reliable
 long-term neural signal recording with minimal cortical tissue damage.
 These findings suggest the potential for long-term stability and decoding
 capabilities of ECoG signals, crucial for chronic human BCI applications
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The major drawback of ECoG, however, is its invasiveness, requiring surgical
 procedures like burr holes or craniotomies for electrode placement, limiting
 its use to specific clinical scenarios or research studies 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_ECoG"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary, ECoG emerges as a highly valuable tool in the realm of BCIs.
 Its exceptional spatial and temporal resolution, coupled with the ability
 to accurately decode a wide array of neural activities, positions it as
 an indispensable asset in advanced BCI research, notwithstanding its invasive
 nature and associated clinical limitations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Non-Invasive Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Electroencephalography (EEG)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Electroencephalography (EEG) has established itself as a vital component
 in the realm of brain-computer interface (BCI) technology, showcasing significa
nt advancements across diverse applications.
 In neuroprosthetics, EEG-based BCIs have revolutionized care for individuals
 with degenerative diseases like ALS, translating neuronal electrical signals
 into actions that enhance independence and mobility.
 This technology extends to communication aids for paralysis patients, where
 scalp-placed electrodes convert brain activity into synthesized speech,
 offering a new communication avenue.
 In the gaming industry, EEG's integration has led to innovative experiences;
 games such as MindRDR and Brain Wars utilize EEG sensors to measure players'
 emotional responses and concentration levels, thereby tailoring the gaming
 experience.
 The educational sector is not left behind, with EEG being used to provide
 real-time cognitive state feedback, aiding in personalized learning and
 stress reduction.
 Additionally, in mental healthcare, EEG's capability for real-time monitoring
 of brain activity presents novel treatment possibilities for conditions
 like depression, anxiety, and addiction 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
EEG's application extends further into sleep medicine, where it plays a
 critical role in enhancing sleep quality and diagnosing sleep-related disorders
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The advent of advanced sensor technologies, including electrochemical transisto
r-based electrodes and nanotechnologies, has notably improved EEG's signal
 quality and practicality 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Neurofeedback-assisted BCIs, offering self-regulation and control over
 specific brain rhythms, have shown promising results in rehabilitation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Despite these advancements, challenges persist, such as the necessity for
 personalized BCI designs tailored to individual brain functions and addressing
 physiological factors that impact BCI performance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-18,key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Technological challenges, including developing more robust features for
 hybrid BCIs, cost and maintenance issues, and addressing socioeconomic
 concerns like ethical considerations and user safety, remain crucial areas
 for ongoing focus 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Key datasets such as DEAP, MAHNOB, DREAMER, GAMEEMO, and SEED, utilized
 by a majority of studies, along with advanced EEG systems like the Biosemi
 ActiveTwo, Emotiv Epoc headset, and Brain Vision LLC's Neuroscan Mobita,
 are fundamental in capturing high-fidelity EEG signals for emotion recognition
 research 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-18"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent advancements in BCI neurofeedback highlight its role in inducing
 brain plasticity, aiding in the repair and control of nervous system outputs
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This aspect of EEG-based BCIs emphasizes their potential in neurorehabilitation
, where they assist in the re-establishment of control through neural prostheses
 and reactivation of impaired synaptic networks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Concurrently, significant strides in sensor design have emerged.
 Innovations like quasi-dry electrodes, which combine the advantages of
 both dry and wet electrodes, reflect a focus on improved signal-to-noise
 ratio, resolution, and user comfort 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Enhanced electrode technologies, such as transistor-based amplification
 and nanowire sensors, have markedly improved EEG signal quality 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Moreover, the advent of hybrid BCI systems, integrating modalities like
 SSVEP/ERP and SSVEP/MI, demonstrates a forward leap in BCI robustness and
 functionality 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Functional Near-Infrared Spectroscopy (fNIRS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Functional near-infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS) stands out as a non-invasive
 brain imaging technique leveraging light to measure changes in oxygenated
 and deoxygenated hemoglobin levels 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This modality offers the capability to monitor both regional and global
 cortical activities, proving beneficial in a range of brain-computer interface
 (BCI) applications such as motor imagery, language processing, and affective
 state recognition 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The setup of fNIRS, involving the number and arrangement of sensors, is
 crucial for the accurate collection of data.
 These sensors allow for the monitoring of multiple brain areas simultaneously,
 providing excellent spatial resolution.
 However, fNIRS faces limitations in temporal resolution when compared to
 EEG, making it less suitable for tasks requiring rapid response detection
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Moreover, the signals obtained through fNIRS are relatively weak and prone
 to noise interference, posing challenges in data interpretation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In terms of practical considerations, fNIRS systems are generally more costly
 than EEG setups and necessitate specialized training for effective use
 and interpretation of results.
 There are also safety considerations due to the exposure to light, which,
 if not properly managed, could lead to potential eye damage 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Despite these challenges, fNIRS remains a valuable tool in the realm of
 neuroimaging and brain research, offering unique insights into brain function
 and activity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Magnetoencephalography (MEG)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Magnetoencephalography (MEG) is a non-invasive technique that measures magnetic
 fields generated by electrical currents in neurons, offering excellent
 temporal resolution 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (≈5,000 Hz 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-21"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 This feature makes MEG particularly useful for tracking dynamic neural
 activities related to cognitive processes such as attention and memory,
 as well as for various brain-computer interface (BCI) applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 A key factor in MEG's accuracy is the density of sensors and their placement,
 which should be tailored to individual anatomy to optimize signal fidelity
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the major advantages of MEG is its good signal-to-noise ratio, providing
 strong and reliable signals that are beneficial for BCI applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It also offers high spatial resolution, enabling accurate mapping of brain
 activity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, the high cost of MEG equipment limits its practicality outside
 specialized research laboratories and hospitals 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This cost factor, although contributing to its high accuracy, poses a significa
nt barrier to more widespread use.
 Additionally, MEG is sensitive to head motion artifacts, which can interfere
 with the accuracy of the recorded signals 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It employs superconducting quantum interference devices (SQUID) for detecting
 magnetic fields, capitalizing on their high sensitivity to neural activity
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite its advantages, MEG-based BCI is still in its infancy and requires
 further research and exploration 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It offers superior spatiotemporal resolution compared to EEG, making it
 particularly useful in epilepsy treatment for identifying seizure foci
 and aiding in surgical planning 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, its sensitivity to external environmental noise remains a challenge
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Overall, MEG represents a valuable tool in neuroimaging, providing unique
 insights into brain activity and function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) has emerged as a pivotal tool
 in neuroimaging, characterized by its superior spatial resolution, a feature
 instrumental in pinpointing active brain sections 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Operating at electromagnetic fields of 3 T or 7 T, fMRI is adept at measuring
 hemodynamic and metabolic activities within the brain, utilizing these
 parameters to identify areas of neural activation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, fMRI's application is constrained by several limitations.
 Notably, it suffers from poor temporal resolution, typically around 1-2
 seconds (≈0.5 Hz), coupled with a physiological delay of 3-6 seconds due
 to the hemodynamic response 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15,key-21,key-22"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This sluggish response hinders its suitability for real-time applications,
 a significant limitation in the context of brain-computer interface (BCI)
 technologies and rapid communication scenarios.
 Furthermore, fMRI's efficacy is compromised by its sensitivity to head
 movements, which can introduce artifacts into the imaging data 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent advancements in artificial intelligence have notably enhanced the
 decoding of brain activity from fMRI data, particularly in visual perception
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-21"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These innovations, while significant, have not overcome the intrinsic limitatio
ns of fMRI, such as its limited temporal resolution.
 Consequently, fMRI remains primarily focused on low-level visual features
 and faces challenges in fully exploiting its potential in real-time application
s and dynamic stimulus decoding, such as speech and videos 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-21"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In summary, while fMRI stands out for its spatial resolution and cost-effective
ness compared to other modalities like PET and EEG, its temporal limitations
 and sensitivity to motion artifacts present significant challenges for
 its broader application in neuroscientific research and clinical settings
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15,key-21"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Positron Emission Tomography (PET)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Positron Emission Tomography (PET), a highly sensitive, non-invasive neuroimagin
g technique, is primarily employed to measure hemodynamic activity related
 to cerebral blood flow.
 This method uses radionuclides embedded in biological molecules, emitting
 positrons to enable the detection of diverse brain activities and abnormalities.
 PET's efficacy is particularly highlighted in its ability to localize specific
 brain regions, as demonstrated in studies involving hand grasping movements,
 showcasing its precision in neuroimaging applications.
 Additionally, its role extends to the molecular imaging of cancer and the
 detection of brain tumors 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the realm of oncology, PET, especially through the use of 18F-fluorodeoxygluc
ose (FDG), is indispensable for tumor segmentation.
 The quantification of metabolic activity via metabolic tumor volume (MTV)
 and total lesion glycolysis (TLG) in PET scans is critical for predicting
 patient outcomes and assessing treatment responses in various malignancies.
 These measures provide valuable insights into the metabolic activity of
 tumors, with higher metabolic activity in pretreatment scans often signaling
 a worse prognosis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Beyond oncology, PET's clinical value also extends to diagnosing dementia
 in Alzheimer's patients, significantly contributing to the understanding
 of neurological disorders.
 This specific application of PET in Alzheimer's diagnosis underlines its
 broader clinical relevance beyond cancer imaging 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Notably, FDG PET's approach to measuring MTV offers distinct advantages
 over traditional anatomic imaging methods like MRI and CT.
 It is easier, faster, and potentially more reflective of the actual shrinkage
 of the viable tumor portion post-chemo- or radiotherapy 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the integration of MTV into standard clinical practice faces challenges
, primarily due to the absence of a consensus on the optimal methods for
 tumor segmentation in FDG PET images.
 This lack of a definitive approach emphasizes the need for ongoing research
 and development in PET imaging for oncology 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-23"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In comparison to EEG, which primarily measures electrical brain activity,
 PET provides a contrasting perspective by focusing on metabolic processes.
 This difference is significant, as combining PET with EEG can yield a more
 comprehensive understanding of brain function and structure 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-17,key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite its advantages, PET's application is limited by certain drawbacks,
 notably its ineffectiveness in obtaining cross-sectional brain images and
 accurately pinpointing the exact locations of tumors or injuries.
 This limitation highlights a critical area where PET can be complemented
 by other imaging techniques 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15,key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Additionally, factors like high operating costs and limited portability
 constrain PET's widespread use, particularly in contrast to more accessible
 techniques like EEG 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-20"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Classical Paradigms in BCI Systems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
P300
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The P300 paradigm is a prominent component in the realm of Brain-Computer
 Interface (BCI) systems, known for its high accuracy and ease of use.
 Developed in the 1980s, P300 leverages the EEG's ability to capture event-relat
ed potentials, particularly useful when a user recognizes specific stimuli.
 This process results in the emergence of the P300 wave, approximately 300
 milliseconds post-stimulus, serving as an indicator of the user's cognitive
 engagement and attention.
 The practicality of P300-based BCIs extends to a wide array of applications,
 including medical rehabilitation, assistive communication, and cognitive
 assessment, among others, thereby highlighting its versatility and significance
 in diverse fields 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the P300 paradigm is not without its challenges.
 One of the primary drawbacks is its susceptibility to noise interference,
 which can adversely affect the quality of data collected.
 Additionally, achieving accurate responses often necessitates a considerable
 number of trials, leading to slower response times.
 This characteristic, in turn, impacts the efficiency and user experience
 of P300-based BCIs 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Regarding the generation of the P300 response, a possible method involves
 Donchin's Speller.
 This system employs an alphanumeric matrix displayed on a computer screen.
 The stimuli are created by randomly flashing rows and columns of the matrix.
 When a user concentrates on a specific cell, the flashing of that cell's
 row or column becomes a significant event, eliciting the P300 response
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_Genetic_3_P300"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Steady-State Visual Evoked Potentials (SSVEP)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
SSVEP Data Collection and Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Steady-State Visual Evoked Potentials (SSVEP) method, a cornerstone
 in EEG-based brain-computer interface (BCI) systems, relies on the rhythmic
 brain responses evoked by visual stimuli at specific flicker frequencies.
 SSVEP exploits these flicker frequencies from display monitors to generate
 EEG patterns that directly correlate with the user's gaze direction, thus
 enabling precise tracking of eye movements 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This capability is achieved as participants focus on changing visual stimuli,
 like colored squares, each eliciting a unique neural signature corresponding
 to the light's frequency.
 This method's robustness lies in its ability to accurately track eye movements
 without the need for additional equipment, such as cameras, making it a
 versatile tool in various applications ranging from virtual reality and
 AI research to medical diagnostics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
SSVEP-based BCIs are noted for their high information transfer rates and
 low training requirements, offering significant advantages over other BCI
 methods such as P300 in terms of response speed and bandwidth, crucial
 for real-time applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_Genetic_1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, they also present challenges, including a lack of flexibility
 and a cumbersome setup process.
 These factors, especially the need for detailed calibration, can affect
 the portability and adaptability of SSVEP systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A notable example in SSVEP research is the Wang Open Dataset presented in
 2017, which includes EEG data from 35 subjects, comprising both experienced
 and naive participants 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This dataset exemplifies the typical SSVEP experimental setup, where subjects
 are exposed to visual stimuli flickering at various frequencies.
 EEG data is initially recorded at a high sampling rate and later downsampled
 for analysis.
 Comprehensive datasets like this are vital for developing and validating
 SSVEP-based BCIs, offering a rich source of data for exploring SSVEP phenomena
 in diverse populations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Other widely used datasets include the Nakanishi Open Dataset from 2015,
 and the BETA Open Dataset from 2020 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Advancements in SSVEP Signal Processing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent advancements in SSVEP signal processing, particularly in the preprocessin
g phase, have significantly leveraged deep learning techniques 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Central to these advancements are the use of time-frequency transforms
 and filter banks, which play a pivotal role in enhancing the quality and
 interpretability of SSVEP signals.
 Filter bank analysis, involving sub-band decompositions with multiple filters
 of varying pass-bands, is especially crucial.
 The integration of techniques like the Filter Bank Canonical Correlation
 Analysis (FBCCA), which combines fundamental and harmonic frequency components,
 has markedly improved SSVEP detection over traditional methods 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Moreover, preprocessing steps like applying frequency filters to mitigate
 noise and implementing time-frequency transforms such as the Fast Fourier
 Transform (FFT) have proven effective in simplifying feature extraction.
 These methods have greatly aided deep learning models in efficiently processing
 SSVEP inputs, thereby making frequency features more accessible and enhancing
 overall model performance in SSVEP classification 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Deep Learning Models in SSVEP BCI
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Deep learning models have become increasingly central in SSVEP-based BCIs.
 These models typically fall into three categories: fully connected neural
 networks, convolutional neural networks (CNNs), and recurrent neural networks
 (RNNs), including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 CNNs, known for their convolution layers, require less computational power
 and are less prone to overfitting, particularly with limited training data
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 RNNs, and LSTMs specifically, are notable for their capacity to remember
 input sequences, making them suitable for applications where the temporal
 dynamics of SSVEP signals are critical 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Motor Imagery (MI)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Motor Imagery (MI), an important component in Brain-Computer Interface (BCI)
 research, involves users mentally simulating motor actions without physical
 movement.
 Rooted in the primary sensorimotor area of the brain, MI mimics the dynamics
 of actual movement 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-12"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Its diverse applications include controlling assistive devices like wheelchairs
, enhancing rehabilitation, and aiding communication for those with speech
 impairments 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An integral aspect of MI is the understanding and leveraging of sensorimotor
 rhythms, specifically the mu (8-13 Hz) and beta (12-30 Hz) rhythms.
 When cerebral activity is associated with motor activity, variations in
 the amplitudes of these rhythms occur, which are essential for MI in BCIs
 since they are not related to actual movement.
 Notably, these sensorimotor rhythms are utilized in BCIs to interpret cognitive
 tasks such as prediction, learning, and multisensory integration 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
MI research has been focusing on enhancing system accuracy and robustness
 through advanced techniques.
 Ensemble classifiers, particularly those using the sub-band common spatial
 pattern (SBCSP) method, are employed to address individual differences
 in rhythmic patterns related to MI.
 Moreover, the incorporation of fuzzy integral and particle swarm optimization
 (PSO) techniques is used for regulating individual differences in parameters,
 thus enhancing the system's overall performance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-12"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, challenges such as distinguishing between real and imagined motor
 tasks and user fatigue impact the performance and accuracy of MI systems
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To enhance MI classification accuracy and speed, extensive research has
 been focused on applying various machine learning algorithms such as KNN,
 LDA, SVM, and ANN 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to MI, Slow Cortical Potentials (SCPs) and non-motor cognitive
 tasks play significant roles in BCI.
 SCPs, characterized by slow voltage shifts in EEG signals, lasting several
 seconds, are indicative of cortical activity variations and are employed
 in various BCI control applications, although they require extensive training
 and usually yield lower information transfer rates (ITRs) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Non-motor cognitive tasks, such as visual counting and mental rotation,
 improve the performance of adaptive BCI systems for users with severe motor
 impairments 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In MI-based BCIs, channel and feature selection are crucial, with techniques
 like modified Stockwell transforms and multilevel hybrid PSO-Bayesian linear
 discriminant analysis being employed for signal classification and optimization.
 These methods reduce the number of features needed and shorten testing
 time, thus enhancing the practicality of MI-BCI systems, particularly for
 patients with motor disabilities 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-11"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
BCI Signal Processing Techniques
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Path Signature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Path Signature is an innovative and robust feature for Brain-Computer Interface
 (BCI) signal processing, offering a novel approach to interpreting electroencep
halography (EEG) signals.
 As a sequence of iterated integrals derived from a multidimensional path,
 the path signature uniquely maintains invariance to translation and time
 reparametrization.
 This robustness is especially advantageous for EEG time series, which frequentl
y suffer from non-stationarity and environmental noise 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-25"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The application of path signature in BCI is driven by its ability to capture
 geometric features of the path.
 The first and second levels of the signature offer an intuitive understanding
 by focusing on the displacement of the path and the relationships between
 pairs of path components, respectively.
 These initial levels are effective in capturing the cyclic order of EEG
 data, which proved to be highly valuable in both intra- and inter-subject
 classifications.
 The higher levels of the signature add further analytical depth by detailing
 more complex features of the EEG signals, thus transforming nonlinear classific
ation challenges into more manageable linear ones 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-25"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In BCI systems, the path signature method stands out for its ability to
 handle the high variability inherent in EEG signals.
 This variability, which stems from differences in sessions and users, poses
 a significant challenge in establishing robust classifiers.
 The path signature method addresses this issue by providing features that
 are more resistant to these variances.
 By combining the path signature with standard classifiers like the Riemannian
 classifier, which leverages the geometric structure of symmetric positive
 definite matrices, there is a noticeable improvement in performance, particular
ly in scenarios involving noisy and low-quality data 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-25"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real-world impact of this method was evident in studies that employed
 the Path Signature as a feature map for EEG signal classification.
 Utilizing n-dimensional paths derived from EEG data, the Path Signature
 method, particularly at its second level, demonstrated effective results.
 This was evident in datasets like the BCI competition IV 2a, where it achieved
 high accuracy in both intra- and inter-subject classifications.
 Particularly notable is its success with subjects less responsive to traditiona
l classifiers, indicating its potential in addressing inter-subject variability
 and reliable EEG signal interpreting 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-25"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dimensionality Reduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dimensionality reduction in Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) systems, particularly
 through Independent Component Analysis (ICA) and Principal Component Analysis
 (PCA), is important for enhancing EEG signal processing.
 ICA is instrumental in segregating mixed signals into independent sources,
 thereby facilitating the extraction of critical information like event-related
 potentials.
 Its utility in artifact removal is significant, reducing false positives
 caused by external interferences such as movement artifacts 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, ICA algorithms face challenges, including ambiguity in source
 origin and reduced efficacy with small datasets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PCA focuses on transforming correlated observations into a set of uncorrelated
 variables.
 This method excels in noise reduction and data dimensionality reduction,
 though it does not inherently ensure effective classification.
 By eliminating noise through the identification of small eigenvalues, PCA
 partially denoises the EEG data, contributing to cleaner signal interpretation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15,key-17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The integration of PCA with other methods like Ensemble Empirical Mode
 Decomposition (EEMD) enhances EEG data quality, particularly in artifact
 suppression 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to ICA and PCA, other advanced techniques like EEMD and Multivariate
 Adaptive Moving Average–Empirical Mode Decomposition (MAMA-EMD) have emerged.
 EEMD, when combined with techniques like Canonical Correlation Analysis
 (CCA), enhances the number of channels available for ICA by leveraging
 interchannel information and addressing challenges in EEG data with low
 signal-to-noise ratio 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 MAMA-EMD, an advancement over EEMD, focuses on extracting spikes into the
 first Intrinsic Mode Function (IMF) to improve the accuracy of ensuing
 IMF screening and mitigate mode-splitting effects, although it may encounter
 difficulties with multi-point spikes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-17"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Additionally, CCA is proficient at extracting inter-band correlation (IBC)
 features based on differential entropy (DE), providing insights into the
 mutual interactions between EEG frequency bands under various emotional
 expressions.
 This method is particularly beneficial as it avoids the need for surrogate
 testing, thus reducing computational complexity.
 In practice, the combination of IBC features with traditional DE features
 at the decision level has been shown to significantly enhance the accuracy
 of emotion recognition 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-24"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These dimension reduction techniques, including ICA, PCA, EEMD, and MAMA-EMD,
 are essential in refining BCI systems.
 They offer distinct yet complementary approaches, contributing to more
 accurate and effective EEG signal processing in BCI research.
 While ICA and PCA are adept at identifying independent sources and reducing
 data noise, respectively, EEMD and MAMA-EMD bring additional capabilities
 in dealing with complex artifacts and improving signal clarity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Wavelet Transformations (WT)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Wavelet Transformations (WT) play a crucial role in Brain-Computer Interface
 (BCI) systems.
 WT uniquely decomposes EEG data into frequency components, which is pivotal
 for analyzing brain signals associated with various cognitive tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This decomposition is essential in understanding how different frequencies
 in the brain relate to specific mental activities, enhancing the capability
 of BCIs to interpret and respond to user inputs effectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically, the Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) is employed to calculate
 the scaling and detail components of a waveform, crucial for EEG signal
 processing.
 DWT's ability to handle artifact removal through the thresholding of decomposed
 coefficients makes it a valuable tool in EEG analysis.
 However, it faces challenges when the spectral properties of the signal
 overlap with those of the artifacts 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To overcome the limitations of DWT, the Stationary Wavelet Transform (SWT)
 is used.
 SWT eliminates temporal dispersion, a drawback of DWT, and allows for the
 tracking of changes in the harmonic components of EEG signals over time
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the broader context of feature extraction in BCIs, Wavelet Transformations
 stand alongside other methods such as Autoregressive Modeling, Continuous
 and Discrete Wavelet Transforms, and Matched Filtering.
 Each of these techniques offers unique advantages for processing and analyzing
 EEG signals, contributing to the development of more accurate and responsive
 BCI systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hidden Markov Models (HMM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hidden Markov Models (HMMs) are integral to BCI systems, primarily for recognizi
ng user intentions through EEG signal analysis.
 HMMs, based on Bayesian posterior probability maximization, are adept at
 classifying time series data, making them suitable for tasks like movement
 intent detection or mental arithmetic classification.
 Central to their functionality are three core problems: evaluation (determining
 the probability of a sequence of visible states generated by the model),
 decoding (identifying the most probable sequence of hidden states), and
 learning (optimizing model parameters from training data).
 Techniques like the Viterbi algorithm and Baum-Welch algorithm are employed
 for these purposes.
 Despite their proven effectiveness in classifying EEG time series and the
 capability to calculate likelihoods of feature vector sequences, HMMs face
 challenges in BCI application.
 A significant hurdle is the identification of stable observable states
 within EEG signals, a task complicated by the intricate nature of brain
 activities and signal dynamics.
 This limitation hinders their widespread adoption in BCI development, suggestin
g a need for further exploration into EEG signals and neurophysiological
 brain functioning to enhance their applicability 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Artificial Neural Networks (ANN)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) are particularly adept at processing and
 classifying brain signals in EEG-based systems.
 ANNs exhibit excellent flexibility and are available in numerous architectures,
 including Probabilistic Neural Networks (PNN), Fuzzy ARTMAP ANN, Finite
 Impulse Response Neural Networks (FIRNN), and Probability Estimating Guarded
 Neural Classifiers (PeGNC).
 These diverse architectures enable ANNs to enhance their adaptability to
 various signal processing tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-15"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are notable for their efficiency in
 managing complex tasks like image classification within EEG data.
 By employing spectrograms as inputs, CNNs simplify the classification process.
 Furthermore, specific architectures like ShallowNet, which employ time
 convolution and spatial filtering, mirror the capabilities of traditional
 methods such as the Filter Bank Common Spatial Pattern (FBCSP).
 This parallelism allows for enhanced processing of EEG signals, thereby
 facilitating accurate signal classification and supporting the rapid decoding
 of complex signals such as hand gestures in electrocorticography 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the successful implementation of these neural networks in EEG-based
 systems is not without challenges.
 One significant hurdle is their dependence on the quantity and quality
 of available data.
 The performance of deep learning methods, like neural networks, improves
 with access to more data, leading to better generalization and accuracy
 in signal interpretation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This data dependency necessitates the use of data augmentation techniques
 to address the issue of limited data availability.
 Augmentation methods artificially expand the training dataset, increasing
 variability and the number of samples.
 This expansion is crucial for training robust neural network models that
 can effectively handle the complexities of EEG signal processing 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SOTA_BCI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Genetic Algorithms (GA)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the realm of Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) systems, the application
 of Genetic Algorithms (GA) has significantly advanced feature selection
 and classification processes.
 Notably, GA has been effectively used in Steady State Visually Evoked Potential
 (SSVEP)-based BCIs for optimizing feature selection, enhancing classification
 accuracy, and reducing the dimensionality of data 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_Genetic_1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Similarly, in motor imagery-based BCI systems, GA coupled with Stockwell
 transform and Bayesian linear discriminant analysis has shown to significantly
 improve classification performance by selecting the most relevant feature
 sets, achieving high sensitivity, specificity, precision, and accuracy
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BCI_Genetic_2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, a study on P300-based BCIs demonstrated the efficacy of GA
