\begin_layout Subsection
EEG Text Recognition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the paper at 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "key-10"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the researchers employed the ZuCo 1.0 and 2.0 datasets for their experiments.
 These datasets recorded text and EEG corpus during Normal Reading (NR)
 and Task-Specific Reading (TSR) tasks, using a 128-channel system at a
 500 Hz sampling rate.
 After noise canceling, 105 channels were used for translation.
 For word-level EEG feature translation, the DeWave model utilized eye-fixation
 data associated with each word during reading, available in the ZuCo dataset.
 The EEG waves corresponding to words fixated upon multiple times were concatena
ted for processing.
 The statistical results of the EEG wave segments were computed across four
 frequency band filters: Theta (5-7Hz), Alpha (8-13Hz), Beta (12-30Hz),
 and Gamma (30Hz and above), resulting in a feature size of 840 for each
 word.
 For decoding purposes, the raw EEG signals were normalized to a range between
 0 and 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
DeWave's training involved a multi-stage process.
 Initially, the language model wasn't included in weight updates, focusing
 on training a proper encoder projection and a discrete codex representation
 for the language model.
 In the subsequent stage, the weights, including those of the language model,
 were fine-tuned.
 The model implementation for word-level EEG features involved using 56
 tokens, each with an 840 embedding size.
 For raw EEG waves, the signals were clipped or padded up to 5500 sample
 points.
 A transformer layer with eight heads and a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times1$
\end_inset

 convolutional layer fused multiple EEG channels into an embedding sequence
 of size 512.
 DeWave used a codex of size 2048, where each latent codex was an embedding
 of size 512.
 The training process involved different learning rates and epochs for self-supe
rvised decoding for raw waves and for training the codex.
 All models were trained on Nvidia V100 and A100 GPUs.
 Performance evaluation was based on Natural Language Processing (NLP) metrics
 like BLEU and ROUGE.
 DeWave's results were compared with existing EEG-to-Text methods, and its
 performance was benchmarked against baselines established for raw EEG waves.
 This comparison involved segmenting entire EEG waves into a sequence embedding
 and using Wave2Vec, adapted for brain waves.
 Additionally, unsupervised raw EEG waves classification methods like BENDR
 and SCL were used for comparison to underscore the impact of discrete encoding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary, the DeWave framework introduces a novel approach to EEG-to-text
 translation, demonstrating its effectiveness through robust performance
 metrics.
 The use of discrete encoding and a multi-stage training process highlights
 the model's innovative approach to translating brain dynamics into text.
\end_layout

