\begin_layout Subsection
Implementation Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section provides a detailed overview of the implementation of the EEG
 emotion recognition experiment.
 The process is divided into several key stages: video preparation and experimen
t execution using the EmotivPRO Builder.
 Each stage is critical for ensuring the smooth operation and accurate data
 collection during the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Video Preparation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The video preparation stage involves two main tasks: concatenation of individual
 video clips and segmentation of the concatenated videos into 4-minute parts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Concatenation of Videos: 
\series default
To begin with, video clips intended to elicit specific emotions (positive,
 neutral, and negative) were collected and stored in separate directories.
 Each directory corresponds to a specific emotion.
 The videos were concatenated into a single video file per emotion to facilitate
 easier playback during the experiment.
 This was achieved using a video processing library which concatenates multiple
 video files into a single continuous video.
 The output video is saved in a predetermined format (MP4), ensuring compatibili
ty with the subsequent processing steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Segmentation into 4-Minute Parts: 
\series default
Once the videos were concatenated, they were further segmented into 4-minute
 parts.
 This segmentation is crucial for maintaining the structure of the experiment
 and ensuring that each trial is of manageable length.
 The segmentation process involves dividing the concatenated video into
 equal parts, each approximately 4 minutes long.
 If a segment is significantly shorter than the desired duration (less than
 90% of the target length), it is discarded to maintain consistency across
 the dataset.
 Both the video and corresponding audio are segmented and saved separately,
 ensuring they can be played back in sync during the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Experiment Execution Using EmotivPRO Builder
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Initially, an OpenCV-based program was developed to handle the experiment
 execution.
 This program included functionalities such as displaying video stimuli,
 collecting participant responses, and managing the flow of the experiment.
 However, due to the requirement for Cortex SDK access, this program was
 ultimately not used.
 Instead, the EmotivPRO Builder platform was utilized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Using EmotivPRO Builder:
\series default
 The EmotivPRO Builder is an online platform designed for creating neuroscience
 experiments with Emotiv's EEG headsets.
 It allows for easy construction of experiments through various phases such
 as calibration, instruction, and stimuli presentation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Experiment Design:
\series default
 Four separate experiments were created using the EmotivPRO Builder, each
 lasting approximately 55 minutes to constitute one session of EEG recording.
 Each experiment began with instructions for the participant, followed by
 headset calibration (15 seconds with eyes open looking at one spot, 15
 seconds with eyes closed).
 Each trial within the experiment included the phases defined in Section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Stimuli and Procedure"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Event Marking:
\series default
 The EmotivPRO Builder automatically marked the EEG recordings with events
 such as the start and stop of each video.
 The order of the emotions presented was positive, negative, neutral, and
 then repeating in this sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This structured approach ensured the collection of high-quality EEG data,
 which formed the basis for subsequent emotion recognition model training
 and evaluation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Functionalities of the OpenCV Program (Not Used)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The OpenCV program, although not used, was designed with several functionalities
 to enhance user experience and ensure accurate data collection.
 These details are included here for completeness:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Experiment Protocol:
\series default
 The experiment followed a structured protocol for each trial, the same
 one defined in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Stimuli and Procedure"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The sequence is designed to maximize participant engagement and ensure
 accurate emotion induction and recording.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Displaying Video and Messages:
\series default
 OpenCV was used to display video stimuli and messages to the participant.
 The video playback was handled within a full-screen window to minimize
 distractions and immerse the participant in the video content.
 Messages, such as hints about the upcoming trial or instructions for self-scori
ng, were displayed using OpenCV’s text rendering capabilities.
 The text was centrally aligned and color-coded based on the type of message
 (e.g., hints, pauses).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
User Interaction:
\series default
 Participants interacted with the system using keyboard inputs.
 Key functionalities included pausing the experiment (space bar), quitting
 (Q key), and skipping to the next screen (S key).
 When the experiment was paused, the screen displayed a paused message,
 and the system waited for the participant to resume.
 The synchronization between the video and audio was maintained throughout
 the experiment to ensure accurate timing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Self-Scoring:
\series default
 After each video clip, participants were prompted to score their emotional
 response.
 The scoring interface was displayed on the screen, and participants used
 the keyboard to input their scores.
 The scores were saved in a structured format for later analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Audio Synchronization:
\series default
 The audio synchronization was managed by a custom audio player class, ensuring
 that the audio and video remained in sync throughout the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Audio Player Implementation:
\series default
 The audio player was implemented using the PyAudio library, which provided
 low-level audio playback functionalities.
 It opened the audio file, read the audio frames, and streamed them to the
 output device.
 The audio player class supported basic operations such as play, pause,
 resume, and seek, allowing for precise control over audio playback.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Synchronization with Video:
\series default
 During video playback, the system continuously monitored the synchronization
 between the audio and video streams.
 If a desynchronization was detected (e.g., audio and video were out of sync
 by more than 250 milliseconds), the audio player re-synced the audio to
 the current video position.
 This ensured that the participant’s experience was not disrupted by timing
 issues.
 In practice, while experimenting, the audio was out of sync for at most
 10 milliseconds even without this synchronization mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Handling Pauses and Skips:
\series default
 When the experiment was paused, the audio playback was also paused to maintain
 synchronization.
 Upon resuming, both the audio and video continued from the same point,
 ensuring continuity.
 If the participant chose to skip a video, the system stopped the current
 playback and proceeded to the next segment, updating the internal state
 accordingly.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Functionalities of the Experiment:
\series default
 The experiment included several functionalities to enhance the user experience
 and ensure accurate data collection.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Pausing the Experiment:
\series default
 Participants could pause the experiment at any time by pressing the space
 bar.
 During the pause, the screen displayed a paused message, and both video
 and audio playback were halted.
 This functionality allowed participants to take breaks without affecting
 the experiment's flow.
 It is important to note that this feature should generally not be used
 during the video playback sections to maintain the integrity of the emotional
 response being recorded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Skipping Segments:
\series default
 Participants could skip to the next video segment by pressing the S key.
 This was useful if a participant felt that the current segment was not
 suitable or if there was an issue with the video playback, or they just
 didn't want to wait anymore.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Automatic Rest Periods:
\series default
 After each self-scoring phase, a rest period was included to allow participants
 to relax and prepare for the next trial.
 This rest period was crucial for maintaining participant engagement and
 preventing fatigue.
 The rest duration was fixed, and the system automatically transitioned
 to the next trial after the rest period ended.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While these functionalities were developed, they were ultimately not employed
 due to the transition to using EmotivPRO Builder for the experiment execution.
\end_layout

