\begin_layout Subsection
Data Augmentation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data augmentation is applied to enhance the diversity of the training dataset
 and improve model robustness.
 The GAN was specifically used on the EmotionWave dataset.
 It was trained on segments of 15 seconds, which with 200 Hz resulted in
 training the GAN to create data of shape 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times3000$
\end_inset

.
 A separate GAN was trained for each of the 14 channels of the EmotionWave
 dataset, which were concatenated into 
\begin_inset Formula $14\times3000$
\end_inset

 data.
 Overall, from the original dataset's size of 5760 EEGs of 1 second with
 dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $14\times200$
\end_inset

, after the GAN augmentation, we now have 11520 EEGs of with the same dimensions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to GAN-based augmentation, specific augmentations are applied
 in the time and frequency domains to simulate variability and improve the
 model's robustness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
GAN-based Augmentation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Fuzzy_GAN"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 utilized Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) to generate synthetic EEG
 data that accurately mimics the distribution of real EEG signals.
 This technique enhances data diversity and contributes to the training
 of more robust models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this study, the GAN model addresses data scarcity and class imbalance
 by generating additional EEG segments that exhibit statistical properties
 similar to the real data.
 These synthetic segments are then incorporated into the training set, thereby
 increasing its size and diversity.
 As a result, the augmented dataset improves the generalization and robustness
 of the emotion recognition model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Time and Frequency Domain Augmentation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to GAN-based augmentation, specific augmentations are applied
 in the time and frequency domains to simulate variability and improve the
 model's robustness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Time Domain Augmentation:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Gaussian Noise Addition:
\series default
 Gaussian noise with a specified standard deviation is added to the EEG
 signals to simulate variability.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Frequency Domain Augmentation:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Fourier Transformation:
\series default
 EEG signals are converted from the time domain to the frequency domain
 using the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Spectral Perturbation:
\series default
 Frequency components are selectively perturbed by:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Zeroing out amplitudes that are below a threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Scaling the others based on a uniform probability matrix and a specified
 perturbation range.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These time and frequency domain augmentations enhance model robustness by
 simulating real-world EEG variabilities.
 Combined with GAN-based augmentation, they significantly improve the training
 dataset's diversity and quality, leading to better emotion recognition
 performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model Architecture
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Model Architecture"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proposed model architecture for EEG-based emotion recognition is designed
 to extract robust and generalizable features from EEG signals by leveraging
 both time and frequency domain information and aligning them in a shared
 latent space.
 This approach aims to improve the model's ability to recognize emotions
 accurately by utilizing the rich information contained in both domains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key components of this architecture include contrastive encoders, data
 augmentation techniques, cross-space projectors, and customized loss functions.
 The contrastive encoders are separate for the time and frequency domains,
 each tasked with extracting meaningful embeddings from the EEG signals.
 Data augmentation techniques are employed to create diverse training samples,
 thereby enhancing the model's robustness.
 Cross-space projectors are utilized to map the domain-specific embeddings
 to a shared latent space, ensuring consistency and alignment between the
 time and frequency domains.
 Customized loss functions are designed for each domain and an alignment
 loss is introduced to maintain consistency across these domains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pre-training
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:ModelArchitecture - PreTraining"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pre-training phase is focused on learning generalizable representations
 from the EEG data without using labeled information.
 This phase initializes the model with features that can effectively capture
 the underlying patterns in EEG signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data augmentation in this phase involves two main techniques.
 In the time domain, Gaussian noise is added to the original time-based
 signals to generate weakly augmented samples.
 This augmentation helps the model learn to recognize the core features
 of the signals despite slight variations.
 In the frequency domain, the original time-based signals are transformed
 into the frequency spectrum using Fourier Transformation.
 Perturbations are then introduced by adding or removing frequency components
 based on a uniform probability matrix, enhancing the model's ability to
 understand frequency-based variations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contrastive encoders consist of two components: the time-based encoder
 (ET) and the frequency-based encoder (EF).
 Both are designed as 2-layer transformers.
 The time-based encoder processes the time-domain signals to produce embeddings,
 while the frequency-based encoder processes the frequency-domain signals
 to generate corresponding embeddings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Cross-space projectors are then used to map these embeddings into a shared
 latent space.
 The projector PT maps time-based embeddings, and the projector PF maps
 frequency-based embeddings to this space.
 This alignment is crucial for ensuring that the features extracted from
 both domains are comparable and can be used jointly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As for loss functions, the time domain loss (LT) and frequency domain loss
 (LF) are using an NT-Xent loss that measures the similarity between augmented
 and original time- and frequency-based signals, respectively.
 Additionally, an alignment loss (LA) is employed to align the time and
 frequency embeddings within the shared latent space, ensuring coherence
 between the two domains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fine-tuning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:ModelArchitecture - FineTuning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the fine-tuning phase, the model is trained with labeled EEG data
 to optimize its performance for emotion recognition.
 This phase also incorporates the spatial relationships between EEG channels,
 leveraging a Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) to enhance spatial feature
 extraction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GCN construction involves creating an adjacency matrix using cosine
 similarity between EEG channels.
 This matrix facilitates the capture of spatial features by representing
 the relationships between different EEG channels.
 The graph convolution process employs K-order Chebyshev graph convolution
 to extract these spatial features more effectively, improving the model's
 understanding of the spatial dynamics within the EEG data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The emotion classifier in this architecture is a 3-layer fully connected
 network.
 It comprises hidden layers with dimensions of 1024 and 128, followed by
 an output layer corresponding to the number of emotion classes.
 The classifier's objective is to achieve accurate emotion classification
 by leveraging the enriched embeddings from both the time and frequency
 domains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Loss functions used in the fine-tuning phase include the cross-space losses
 (LT, LF, LA), which are NT-Xent losses applied similarly as in the pre-training
 phase to ensure continuity in learning.
 Additionally, a classification loss, specifically the cross-entropy loss,
 is used for training the emotion classifier to minimize the error in predicting
 the correct emotion classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
GAN Architecture
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:ModelArchitecture - GAN Architecture"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GAN models used in this study consist of a Generator and a Discriminator,
 each designed with specific layers to effectively model the EEG data.
 The Generator is responsible for producing synthetic EEG signals from random
 noise, while the Discriminator evaluates the authenticity of the generated
 signals.
\end_layout

