\begin_layout Subsubsection
Discriminator Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Discriminator architecture is similar to the Generator, but without
 the random tensor input and up-scaling layer.
 The Discriminator processes real and synthetic EEG data to classify them
 as authentic or generated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Rationale for Design Choices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The design of the GAN models incorporates convolutional layers to extract
 frequency signals and an LSTM layer to learn temporal information.
 This combination allows the model to effectively capture the complex spatiotemp
oral patterns in EEG data.
 More specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
MaxAbsScaler:
\series default
 MaxAbsScaler was used to normalize the EEG data because it scales each
 feature by its maximum absolute value, preserving the sparsity of the data
 and ensuring that the GAN operates within a stable range.
 This normalization is particularly important for EEG data due to its wide
 dynamic range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
LSTM Layer:
\series default
 The LSTM layer is critical for capturing the temporal dependencies in the
 EEG signals.
 By learning the temporal patterns, the GAN can generate more realistic
 sequences that reflect the inherent temporal structure of the EEG data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Training and Optimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GAN is trained using Binary Cross-Entropy Loss (BCELoss).
 The optimizers used for both the Generator and Discriminator are Adam Optimizer
s with learning rates of 2e-4 and betas (0.5, 0.999).
 Learning rate scheduler, ReduceLROnPlateau with a patience of 10 epochs,
 is employed for the Generator to adjust the learning rate dynamically,
 ensuring efficient training.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This detailed architecture and training strategy ensure that the GANs effectivel
y generate high-quality synthetic EEG data, which is then used to augment
 the training dataset for the emotion recognition model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model Training
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Model Training"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The training process involves two main phases: pre-training and fine-tuning.
 The model was trained on 70% of the original data, and in cases where data
 was generated with the GAN, it was all added into the training data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pre-training Phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the pre-training phase, the model begins by preparing the dataset, where
 unlabeled EEG data is augmented and fed into the contrastive encoders.
 The loss calculation involves computing the time contrastive loss, frequency
 contrastive loss, and alignment loss using NT-Xent.
 These losses guide the model to learn representations that are robust and
 generalizable.
 The model parameters are then updated via backpropagation using the combined
 loss function, progressively improving the model's feature extraction capabilit
ies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fine-tuning Phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fine-tuning phase starts similarly with data preparation, where labeled
 EEG data is augmented and processed through the pre-trained encoders.
 It additionally passes through a K-order Chebyshev graph convolution to
 extract spatial features using the constructed adjacency matrix.
 The emotion classifier is then trained with cross-entropy loss to fine-tune
 the model for accurate emotion recognition.
 The overall fine-tuning loss, which includes the NT-Xent losses and cross-entro
py loss, is used to update the model parameters.
 This comprehensive training approach ensures that the model is well-optimized
 for recognizing emotions in EEG data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model Evaluation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Model Evaluation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model evaluation involved calculating the evaluation loss using the
 NT-Xent loss on 30% of the original data.
 It is important to note that the evaluation was performed on the original
 augmented data without the GAN data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evaluation Metrics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evaluation metrics included a simple accuracy calculation.
 The accuracy was determined by checking each prediction against the true
 labels, summing the correct predictions, and dividing by the total number
 of samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experimental Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experimental setup involved using the pre-trained model, fine-tuning
 it with the labeled data, and evaluating it on a separate dataset.
 The hardware and software configurations included:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Hardware
\series default
: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 TI GPU.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Software
\series default
: Python 3.12, PyTorch 2.3.0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments were conducted over multiple epochs, with the best-performing
 model based on validation accuracy being selected for final evaluation.
\end_layout

