LatexCommand label
name "fig:pretraining-seed-loss"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pre-training phase of our model using the SEED dataset involved monitoring
 the learning rate and loss over epochs to evaluate the performance and
 effectiveness of our training strategy.
 The learning rate scheduler utilized was 
\series bold
ReduceLROnPlateau
\series default
, which dynamically reduces the learning rate when the monitored metric
 (in this case, the loss) stops improving.
 The patience parameter for the scheduler was set to 50, meaning the learning
 rate was reduced after 50 epochs without significant improvement in the
 loss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As observed in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:pretraining-seed-lr"

\end_inset

, the learning rate remained steady at 4e-4 for the initial 753 epochs.
 However, at epoch 754, due to the lack of improvement in the loss, the
 scheduler reduced the learning rate to 3e-5.
 This reduction in learning rate allowed the model to fine-tune the weights
 more precisely, leading to a noticeable improvement in the loss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Correspondingly, Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:pretraining-seed-loss"

\end_inset

 illustrates the change in the loss over epochs.
 Initially, the loss decreases gradually, showing a general trend of improvement.
 However, it is evident that around epoch 754, there is a marked improvement
 in the loss reduction rate, coinciding with the reduction in the learning
 rate.
 This improvement suggests that the model benefited from a lower learning
 rate, enabling it to escape potential local minima and continue to optimize
 effectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sharp decrease in loss post-epoch 754 highlights the importance of adaptive
 learning rate schedules in training deep learning models.
 By reducing the learning rate, the model can achieve a more stable convergence,
 as seen in the sustained decrease in loss following the adjustment.
 This adaptive approach ensures that the model does not overfit early on
 and continues to learn from the data throughout the training process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the 
\series bold
ReduceLROnPlateau
\series default
 scheduler in enhancing model performance during pre-training.
 The careful adjustment of learning rates in response to the monitored loss
 metric significantly contributes to the model's ability to learn robust
 and generalizable features from the EEG data.
 The pre-training process took approximately 22 hours to complete, indicating
 the computational effort and time investment required to achieve these
 results.
\end_layout

