 improvements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Comparative Analysis
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Comparative Analysis"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section provides a detailed comparison of the performance of EEG-based
 emotion recognition models trained on the SEED dataset and the EmotionWave
 dataset.
 The analysis focuses on evaluating both the pre-training and fine-tuning
 phases, with a specific emphasis on comparing models with and without GAN
 augmentation during pre-training.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pre-training Phase Comparison
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the SEED dataset, the pre-training phase exhibited a consistent decrease
 in loss, demonstrating effective optimization facilitated by adaptive learning
 rate adjustments.
 The learning rate for the SEED dataset was reduced at epoch 754, which
 resulted in a significant improvement in loss reduction.
 This adaptive strategy proved to be effective, ensuring the model could
 continue optimizing without overfitting.
 The entire pre-training phase for the SEED dataset took approximately 22
 hours to complete, reflecting the substantial computational effort required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast, the EmotionWave dataset showed different characteristics in
 its pre-training phase.
 When trained without GAN augmentation, the model demonstrated a steady
 decrease in loss with an earlier learning rate reduction at epoch 613,
 indicating faster convergence and more stable learning.
 However, when GAN augmentation was incorporated, the model exhibited a
 slower rate of loss reduction, with the learning rate adjustment occurring
 later at epoch 1064.
 This slower convergence suggests that the added complexity from GAN augmentatio
n presented optimization challenges.
 The pre-training phase for the EmotionWave dataset took about 1 hour, primarily
 due to the smaller size of the dataset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fine-tuning Phase Comparison
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the fine-tuning phase, the SEED dataset continued to show robust
 performance.
 The training loss decreased continuously, reflecting effective model optimizati
on.
 The evaluation loss stabilized with slight fluctuations, suggesting that
 the model generalized well to unseen data.
 The SEED model achieved an evaluation accuracy of 74.13%, indicating a substanti
al improvement from the pre-training phase and underscoring the model's
 capability to adapt and learn effectively from the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the EmotionWave dataset, the fine-tuning phase presented more challenges.
 When the model was pre-trained without GAN augmentation, it exhibited a
 consistent decrease in training loss, but the evaluation loss showed fluctuatio
ns, indicating potential overfitting.
 Despite these fluctuations, the peak evaluation accuracy reached 66.45%
 at epoch 5 when fine-tuning with GAN data, demonstrating that GAN data
 provided some benefit during fine-tuning.
 However, when the model was pre-trained with GAN augmentation, the training
 loss improved continuously, but the evaluation loss again showed fluctuations.
 This configuration resulted in lower peak accuracy compared to the non-GAN
 configuration, suggesting that the complexities introduced by GAN data
 during pre-training were not fully resolved during fine-tuning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparative analysis reveals that the SEED dataset benefited from a
 more stable training process and achieved higher accuracy during fine-tuning
 compared to the EmotionWave dataset.
 The stability and higher accuracy of the SEED model during fine-tuning
 indicate that the dataset's quality and the pre-training strategies were
 more effective in ensuring robust generalization.
 In contrast, the EmotionWave dataset faced challenges with evaluation loss
 stability and consistency in peak accuracy.
 While the introduction of GAN augmentation increased data diversity and
 provided some benefits, it also added complexity to the training process,
 which affected the optimization dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These findings underscore the critical importance of dataset quality and
 the need for a balanced approach to data augmentation.
 Effective optimization requires not only robust data augmentation strategies,
 but also careful management of the complexities they introduce.
 Future work should focus on enhancing the preprocessing techniques to further
 reduce artifacts in the dataset and improving GAN training methods to ensure
 balanced progress between the generator and discriminator.
 Additionally, exploring more advanced data augmentation and preprocessing
 strategies could improve the overall quality and robustness of emotion
 recognition models.
\end_layout

